# Example 15: arrows showing which parts moved, and how far.
# Checkpoint state 7Z4L to catalytic state 7Z4J, Pacesa et al. 2022,
# Nature 609:191. Arrows run from each domain's start centroid to its end
# centroid, drawn as a cylinder shaft plus a cone head.
#
# This is the still-figure counterpart to the morph in example 12. Use an
# arrow figure when the reader needs to measure the motion, a morph when
# they need to feel it. A paper usually wants both: the arrows in the
# figure, the morph in the supplement.
#
# Three things make an arrow figure honest, and all three are easy to get
# wrong:
#
#   1. Superpose on something that does not move. Arrows are always
#      relative to the reference. Here the superposition is on the RuvC
#      core, so every arrow means "relative to RuvC". Say which reference
#      a figure used, because a different one gives different arrows from
#      identical coordinates.
#   2. Draw arrows at true length. Scaling them up for visibility turns a
#      figure into a cartoon. If a motion is too small to see, print the
#      distance instead.
#   3. Do not draw short arrows at all. Below roughly 1.5 A the direction
#      is coordinate error, but a reader reads any arrow as a real
#      direction. draw_arrows.py drops those and logs them instead.
open 7z4l
open 7z4j
open ../../palettes/lab_colors.cxc

delete #1/A,C,D
delete #2/A,C,c,D

# Superpose on the RuvC core only, so the arrows measure motion relative
# to it rather than a compromise fit over the whole protein.
matchmaker #2/B:1-59,909-1098 to #1/B:1-59,909-1098

open ../../presets/Base/publication_white.cxc

# Start state solid, end state ghosted behind it. The arrow carries the
# direction, so the second copy only needs to establish the destination.
color #1/B lab_gray_p
color #2/B lab_gray_p
transparency #1 55
transparency #2 88

# Colour the moving parts in the start state only
color #1/B:775-908 lab_hnh
color #1/B:906-918 lab_vermillion
color #1/B:497-713 lab_rec3
color #1/B:180-307 lab_rec2

cartoon style xsection oval width 1.2 thickness 0.35

runscript draw_arrows.py

view #1
turn y -30
view #1 pad 0.08

2dlabels text "checkpoint to catalytic" xpos 0.03 ypos 0.95 size 22 color lab_black bold true
2dlabels text "7Z4L to 7Z4J, superposed on RuvC" xpos 0.03 ypos 0.91 size 15 color lab_gray
2dlabels text "HNH" xpos 0.03 ypos 0.84 size 18 color lab_hnh bold true
2dlabels text "L1 / L2 linkers" xpos 0.03 ypos 0.80 size 18 color lab_vermillion bold true
2dlabels text "REC2" xpos 0.03 ypos 0.76 size 18 color lab_rec2 bold true
2dlabels text "REC3" xpos 0.03 ypos 0.72 size 18 color lab_rec3 bold true
2dlabels text "arrows at true length, start to end centroid" xpos 0.03 ypos 0.04 size 15 color lab_gray

save motion_arrows.png width 3000 supersample 3
